efore, our parabola has its vertex at $(0,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186249877 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115911" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1071 "What are the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola $(y-1)^{2}=5(x+7)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9ec04397-a1d2-5a43-be90-9c3301fd7dc0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250508 #edb/ref 1074 "The standard equation of a left- or right-opening parabola that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ is\n\n$$\n(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nWe can rewrite the given equation as \n\n$$\n(y-1)^{2}=4(\\frac{5}{4})(x+7).\n$$\n\nIt is now clear that our equation is of the standard type with $p=\\frac{5}{4}.$ \n\nComparing our equation with the standard equation, we can see that we have $h=-7$ and $k=1.$ Therefore, the coordinates of the vertex of the parabola are $(-7,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250951 #edb/ref 1080 "(-6,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250951 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250951 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2e81d39f-516e-5368-bc2d-95dfd56ecc89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250952 #edb/ref 1080 "(1,0)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250952 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250952 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2ed39256-683e-5ef6-a893-2ffd1ac7cfd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250997 #edb/ref 1080 "(6,6)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250997 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186250997 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "351611be-b89c-5259-9da5-647589f8ff53" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251029 #edb/ref 1080 "(-2,-6)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251029 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186251029 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "39bbb12e-0b58-56b1-9b